<p>Friedrich Nietzsche: The Genealogy of Morality</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/friedrich-nietzsche-in-1869.jpg.webp" alt="Photograph of Friedrich Nietzsche, c. 1869." width="860" height="340" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Every thinker we have studied so far has tried to give morality a foundation. Nietzsche asks whether morality deserves one.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    What this unit is about
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>By the time you reach Nietzsche, you have seen four serious attempts to ground morality in something stable. Aristotle grounded it in human nature and the telos of the good life. Aquinas grounded it in eternal and natural law, rooted in God's reason ordering creation. Kant grounded it in the rational structure of the will, independent of nature and history. Mill grounded it in the observable facts of human happiness and suffering. Each account is different, but each shares an assumption: that morality has a foundation, that it is not simply a historical accident or a disguised expression of something less dignified.</p>
<p>Nietzsche rejects the assumption. His question in the <em>Genealogy of Morality</em> is not "what is the correct moral theory?" but "how did our moral concepts come to be the way they are, and what does their history reveal about their worth?" When Nietzsche traces the genealogy of the concepts "good" and "evil," he claims to find something unsettling: that these concepts did not emerge from insight into moral truth, from reason, or from compassion. They emerged from resentment. The weak, unable to overcome the strong by force, overcame them by redefining strength as evil and weakness as goodness. The victory of this "slave morality" is, on Nietzsche's account, one of the most consequential events in Western history, and one of the most philosophically interesting.</p>
<p>You do not have to agree with Nietzsche. Some of his claims are historically dubious, many are deliberately provocative, and his writing is aphoristic and unsystematic by design. What you do have to engage with is the underlying challenge: if our moral vocabulary has a particular history rooted in particular interests and power relations, what follows? Does the origin of a moral concept have any bearing on its validity? Is Nietzsche giving us a reason to abandon morality, to revise it, or simply to understand it more honestly?</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>← LOOKING BACK &nbsp;|&nbsp; LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>The four thinkers before Nietzsche disagreed about where morality comes from but agreed that it comes from somewhere stable: nature, reason, God, or the structure of human well-being. Nietzsche's genealogical method is designed to destabilize all of those answers by showing that what we call moral truth has a history, and that history is not flattering. Unit 6 will take up Simone de Beauvoir, who shares Nietzsche's rejection of fixed moral essences but tries to build a positive ethics out of that rejection rather than stopping at the critique.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    Before you begin
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Nietzsche is the most stylistically demanding writer in this course. He does not argue the way philosophers usually argue: building premises toward a conclusion, anticipating objections, seeking systematic coverage. He writes in aphorisms, extended metaphors, polemical asides, and sudden reversals. He is deliberately unsettling. Some passages are written to provoke rather than to persuade, and distinguishing provocation from argument is part of the work of reading him.</p>
<p>The <em>Genealogy</em> is more structured than much of his writing, which is one reason it works well in an introductory course. The Preface tells you what kind of inquiry you are in for. The First Treatise traces the historical emergence of "good" and "evil" as moral categories and argues that this emergence reveals something troubling about the moral tradition we have inherited. Reading both carefully will give you what you need for the lecture pages, the reflection prompt, and the quiz.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>A NOTE ON THE TRANSLATION</p>
<p>Our reading uses Ian Johnston's translation, which is available open-access. Johnston's English is clear and reads well. Where Nietzsche's German is deliberately harsh or colloquial, Johnston preserves the edge rather than softening it into academic prose. If you encounter a passage that seems blunt or even offensive, that tone is usually intentional on Nietzsche's part.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
