<p>All of our Nietzsche reading comes from the opening sections of <em>On the Genealogy of Morality</em>, the book Nietzsche considered his most systematic work. Ian Johnston's translation is free to read online; the link opens its table of contents.</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://web.viu.ca/johnstoi/nietzsche/genealogytofc.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Nietzsche, <em>On the Genealogy of Morality</em></strong></a> (1887), Preface and First Treatise</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><strong>Preface</strong>: Nietzsche's account of what prompted the inquiry and what kind of inquiry it is</li>
<li><strong>First Treatise</strong>: "Good and Evil," "Good and Bad" — the genealogy of our central moral categories</li>
</ul>
